 // briefly, retake it. IRQL stays raised for the whole wait, because the KeRaiseIrql below is
 // annotated "Make sure we don't suspend" and that is load-bearing - SuspendTrigger ->
 // SyncCapture needs every CPU to run a DPC, so a thread at DISPATCH_LEVEL is what prevents a
 // suspend landing while a request is in flight.
 #define XENBUS_STORE_SPIN_US    100 // stall this long per poll, with the lock DROPPED
 
+// A REQUEST THAT NEVER COMPLETES MUST NOT BE SILENT, AND MUST NOT BE ENDLESS.
+//
+// With the lock dropped (above) a stuck request no longer freezes the rest of the guest, but
+// the caller itself is stuck at DISPATCH_LEVEL, and you cannot wait indefinitely there: the
+// DPC watchdog will eventually bugcheck with a nameless 0x133 no matter what this code does.
+// So the choice is not crash-or-not, it is OUR bugcheck - which logs the request and leaves a
+// dump naming xenstore - or Windows' anonymous one. The quiet alternative, returning an error,
+// is worse than both: callers such as xenvif's FrontendWaitForBackendXenbusStateChange discard
+// the status, so the guest would silently never get a network device, and a request already
+// partly in the ring cannot be withdrawn without desynchronising the stream permanently.
+//
+// BUT IT IS TUNABLE, BECAUSE THE THRESHOLD IS A GUESS. A normal round trip is sub-millisecond;
+// 15 s is four orders of magnitude above that and should mean "broken, not busy". Should,
+// because a heavily loaded host with a backed-up xenstored is exactly the condition this code
+// exists to survive, and a false positive here costs the user a crashed guest. So:
+//   - warnings start at XENBUS_STORE_WARN_S and repeat every second, naming the request, so a
+//     near-miss is visible in the log LONG before anything dies and real latencies can be
+//     measured in the field rather than guessed at here;
+//   - "StoreRequestTimeout" in the driver's Parameters key overrides the 15 s, and 0 DISABLES
+//     the bugcheck entirely, restoring the original wait-forever behaviour for anyone who
+//     would rather have a hung guest than a crashed one.
+#define XENBUS_STORE_WARN_S             5   // start complaining here
+#define XENBUS_STORE_TIMEOUT_S_DEFAULT  15  // and bugcheck here, unless overridden
+
 static PXENBUS_STORE_RESPONSE
 StoreSubmitRequest(
     IN  PXENBUS_STORE_CONTEXT   Context,
     IN  PXENBUS_STORE_REQUEST   Request
     )
 {
